# sdinretro: sdinthrough development retrospective

"Let me make a small coding challenge as my next blog post", I said to myself one slow day.
It should take only a few days; this will be a nice demo!
I wrote a small todo list, and, as usual, started hacking on it as a side project every dawn for about 30 minutes, whether I felt like it or not.
A few days grew into a few weeks, a few weeks into a few months.
Only 18 months later did I manage to clear my todo list and publish the @/sdinthrough and @/sdin blog posts.
I went a bit overboard with the challenge's subject: I created sdin, a configuration language focused on being easy to work with from code.
Talk about scope creep.
What was I even thinking?
Well, actually, I'd been ruminating about all this for over a decade, ever since my first job.

## Motivations

My first six years in software were spent on a team that was responsible for a large-scale deployment of a service.
Our configuration was very complex, and it was in a bespoke configuration language that we abused to the extreme, as predicted by the ``https://mikehadlow.blogspot.com/2012/05/configuration-complexity-clock.html|Configuration Complexity Clock``.
This work left a big mark on me.
I've kept thinking about configuration languages ever since.
No configuration language felt right.
I wanted to have something that is easy on humans, easy on tooling writers, and somehow pushes logic-writing to high-level languages.
I kept experimenting with various small DSLs, but I never really completed anything.

Since high school, I've enjoyed implementing solutions to LeetCode-style problems.
The problem is: I suck at advanced algorithms and hate math.
I just like coding up relatively simple things.
But 90% of these challenges are too abstract.
I finish the challenge, and I don't really get anything useful out of it.
I found some long-form, complex challenges, but there I usually lose the quick correctness tests, so it isn't as exciting anymore.
So I often mulled over what the ideal coding challenge would be for me.

A few years ago I decided I'd make at least one post on my blog every month.
It forces me to keep busy in my free time; it makes me avoid doing nothing and becoming depressed.
My cure for my depression and anxiety was to eliminate the time for them.
As I was looking for content ideas a while ago, I decided to make a small, interactive coding challenge given that it was something I liked doing.
What should the coding challenge be about?
Implementing a new configuration language!
After all, it was the other big topic I had been contemplating for a decade by then!
That seemed to be the perfect mix of algorithmic, data structure, and implementation challenges for me.

## Keeping the momentum

So I started working on this project, and oh boy, its scope ballooned quickly.
But once I commit to doing a blog project, I finish it.
In the past, I struggled a lot with completing stuff, so I adopted the no-zero-days-until-todo-zero philosophy to fix this.
Now I have my own daily morning habit to always make progress, no matter how small.
Some days were hard and I had no motivation, but I still did some easier tasks, and then I quickly regained momentum.
Eventually I ran out of tasks on my todo list.
This is the philosophy that helped me to push through this project over the past 1.5 years.

This project was not related to my day-to-day job in any way.
Each morning I spent about 30 minutes on it (a pomodoro session's worth) before my family woke up and work started.
Mornings work better for me because they are easier to plan, and by the evening I'm usually quite tired.
I used to be tired in the mornings too, but I decided that it doesn't make sense to feel tired after sleep, and after a while my body got the message and adapted.
Now, mornings are my most productive time of day.

## Local testing

The bulk of the project's effort was spent on coming up with the right testcases, and then implementing the solutions for them.
I usually dread testing, but thanks to @/difftesting, it was a breeze for this project.
I set up the testcase inputs and used https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/ypsu/effdump to verify each commit.

Basically I committed the hash of the testcase inputs and testcase outputs my solver generated.
Whenever there was a diff in the hash, I verified the diff of the testcase outputs, updated the hash, and committed.
The hash was maintained with my https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/ypsu/efftesting package, so it wasn't a hassle to keep it up to date.

I recommend checking out the two videos I have at @/difftesting to see how this workflow works.
Since recording the videos, I've made a few more usability improvements to both packages, so the workflow is now even more streamlined.

I love this diff-testing workflow.
After adding and committing the testcases, I can work on the codebase without ever needing to touch tests again other than visually inspecting whether the test results look right or not.
In fact, the UI of sdinthrough shows only diffs, too.
It's not an accident: I wanted to give others a taste of this kind of development workflow.

## User testing

Once the project took shape, I managed to convince a few work friends to try it while I watched them.
It turned out to be a bit of a mess.
Everything about the project was confusing.
The setup, the harness, the initial testcases.
But I made notes about every little gap people hit.
I fixed them and tested again, then made notes and fixes again.
I had a dozen such iterations until the current shape took form.

Without user testing, this project would be unusable and I wouldn't even know why.
Seeing others use my product not only helped improve it but also motivated me.
It's exciting to see people use the product; it makes me want to fix up the rough edges and see their reaction again.
That's why the readme now invites people to share their write-ups or videos of solving the challenge.
I get to see how others approach it, and the best ones get featured in the readme.

## sdin's evolution

A lot of things changed over time in the language, even the name.
Originally I called it "dnot" as in "data notation" because I thought I would keep it very simple with very limited function-calling syntax.
The "functions" (which evolved into today's forms) were similar to lists without any tagname:

```
# Field names didn't require colons.
BuildCommand {
	Binary (getenv CC 'clang')  # Func name was the first value in the func-list.
	Options {
		# Field names could be any bare token or string.
		-c          opt
		'--output' 'hello'
	}
	Args    ['hello.c']
	Comment '''(raw strings)'''
}
```

The released version looks like this:

```
BuildCommand: {
	Binary: getenv(CC default:'clang')
	Options: map(
		-c         opt
		'--output' 'hello'
	)
	Args:    ['hello.c']
	Comment: '''|raw strings'''
}
```

Notice the old version lacked colons.
My AST node was simpler and didn't have a separate Name field.
I treated structs, lists, and functions the same, so that the implementation was simple.
Structs had to have 2*n children to ensure every key had a pair.

But using this API was terrible.
It was annoying to carefully index even indices for the names and then add checks that the name is in identifier form (that's what I needed in 99% of the cases).
And the flat syntax had the confusing/arbitrary rule that it worked only for identifier-like names.
So after a while I decided: to hell with all this, let's just restrict names to identifiers.
Might as well merge the field names and values into a single node.
So my AST node gained a name field.
But what do I do with the after-name tokens?
Do I make my AST node even more complex to accommodate them?
Nah, I rarely use that; let's keep the AST node simple by not allowing comments between the name and the value in the first place!

Forms (sdin's function-call-like syntax) were inspired by the Unix command line interface because a command line is just a simple list of strings, yet it can express complexity succinctly.
Originally I restricted the form contents such that the elements could be only bare tokens or strings, not lists, structs, or other forms.
This was in order to limit complexity: forms should remain simple, so it's always easy to implement them.
But as I was working with my own API for implementing the @/sdinthrough challenge solutions, I found this unnecessarily restrictive.
And forms were way too "different" from the rest of the AST; I didn't like them.
How about we make them like the rest of the nodes?
So I made that happen.
And I moved the tagname before the paren so that it looks more modern.

But what about names in forms?
How can I distinguish names from values?
I realized this would be simpler if I had colons right after the name part.
I thought hard about this, and found that it would make forms really convenient, so it might well be worth it.
So after some tough refactoring, I added colons everywhere, in both forms and structs.
It meant I couldn't have tokens like `host:port` or `2006-01-02.15:04` anymore.
I thought about making some special cases where the colon would still be part of the token, but I decided against it to keep things simple.

Multiline comments? Added, then removed because I didn't like them.
And so on.
I kept tweaking the syntax, sometimes needing massive refactors, until I found it convenient enough.

## sdinthrough's evolution

The challenge harness also went through many iterations and dead ends.
Initially the testcase data was passed to the user's solver in my https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/ypsu/textar format.
But I saw my trusted testers struggling to parse it even before they got to the first challenge.
It required a non-trivial amount of explanation too.
So I switched to a format that is both simple to explain and simple to implement and saw that it was good.

Initially I didn't have task-specific documentation either.
I wanted the challenge to be an adversarial challenge where the requirements are revealed through the testcases.
But this meant that people had to restructure and refactor as they progressed.
Which was also sort of my goal: a challenge that tests people's feature-anticipating and refactoring skills.
But in the user testing I quickly learned people didn't really enjoy this.
I didn't enjoy this either, as I rewrote the formatter part about six times before I found a nice implementation.
So I ended up adding task documentation, and the user experience was much smoother.
I did leave the schema and the formatter tasks underdocumented just in case somebody does want the adversarial-testcases experience (totally not because I'm too lazy to write the docs!!1!).

## AI usage

I didn't use any AI at all for the majority of the project.
Occasionally I brainstormed a bit with AI about some feature, rubber-duck style, but that was it.

But in the last four months, I realized AI had become very good, and I started asking AI for reviews.
I wrote a bespoke tool that concatenates the readme, the go doc, the commit, and the commit diff into a string, and sends that to Gemini's API endpoint since there's some freebie quota for all Google users.
Then I asked for an AI review before each commit.
AI's response was super useful.
It caught typos, bugs, etc.
It would have been silly to ignore this review.
I still wrote everything in my no-autocomplete environment; AI just provided reviews.
No code or testcases were written by it.

I worked on these three sdin blog posts the same way.
I braindumped my thoughts into some sort of semi-coherent structure and asked AI what it thought of it.
Here, the AI was even more useful.
Sometimes it suggested rewriting some parts, and oh boy, its rewrite was so much better than my clunky writing that I couldn't ignore it.
Some people need glasses to see and crutches to walk; I need AI to write coherently.
So yeah, some parts of these blog posts were wordsmithed with AI.
I'm a bit saddened by this, but I didn't want clunky writing to get in the way of the ideas I want to impart through these posts.

AI's largest effect was on the intro section of @/sdin.
Originally, the intro just said "hey, I made an educational config language, here are the features".
And the AI was like "no no no, nobody will care, make it more confident, start with the strongest, most unique traits, don't hedge" etc.
So now the document reads like it is solving world hunger.
It's more interesting than my original low-energy version, so I'm glad the AI called me out on that.

In August I went a bit further: I asked AI to solve my challenge.
I bought a Claude subscription for this because it can code and run tests in its own cloud, so I don't need to run anything locally or set up VMs.
It did manage to solve it, but it uncovered contradictory documentation, testcase pacing issues, etc.
So I fixed those based on its feedback and kept asking it to re-solve my challenge from scratch until it seemed smooth enough.
One full solution takes about 2 hours for Claude, and costs about $50 based on the built-in cost estimator.
I asked it to solve it a dozen times, so I wouldn't be surprised if I racked up several hundred dollars in costs even though I only paid $20 for the month.
I guess the idea is that most people don't fully utilize their subscription, so heavy users like me get subsidized by them.
I was a bad deal for Anthropic, haha.

Another nice thing I noticed with AI is that it can help me regain motivation for the project.
I can just point it to my codebase and ask it "is this reasonable?"
Chances are, it will find something good about the project, which acts as a pep talk and motivates me to figure out the next step and continue with the work.

## What I'd do differently

Honestly, not much.
A lot of work was thrown away in the end (for instance, I rewrote the formatter about six times), but I believe that's an unavoidable part of exploration.
Probably I'd use more AI in the sense that I'd enable AI code review from the get-go, and I would have more rubber-duck sessions with AI to find the right syntax earlier (I would still refuse to use AI to generate code or tests, though).
I feel this was the type of project that required a lot of experimentation and time to get things right.
I don't think I'd be this happy with the syntax, had I rushed through the project too quickly.

This is not my first long-form side project for this blog, so I guess it makes sense that I've got the hang of the process of finishing stuff, and there's not much left to optimize.

